function ArticlePage({ onBack }: { onBack: () => void }) {
  return <div className="stack"><button className="btn btn-small self-start" onClick={onBack}><Icon name="back" className="h-4 w-4" /> Back to Library</button><article className="app-card"><StatusPill tone="blue">Banking Excellence</StatusPill><h1 className="mt-3 max-w-[760px] text-[28px] font-medium leading-tight sm:text-[34px]">Letters of credit: how the documentary principle protects both sides</h1><p className="mt-3 max-w-[720px] text-base text-muted">Why banks pay against documents rather than goods, and what makes a presentation compliant.</p><div className="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-3 text-xs text-muted"><span>AB Ignite Learning</span><span>7 min read</span><span>Intermediate</span></div><div className="article-body mt-8"><p>A letter of credit is a promise by a bank to pay a beneficiary when the documents presented meet the terms and conditions set out in the credit.</p><p>The documentary principle separates the bank’s review from the underlying goods or service. The bank checks documents, not whether the goods are physically perfect. That separation gives each party a predictable process.</p><h2 className="mb-3 mt-8 text-xl font-medium">What reviewers look for</h2><p>Start with the applicant’s instructions, then check the description, dates, amounts, transport documents and required certificates. A discrepancy is not automatically a failure, but it does need to be identified and handled clearly.</p><div className="mt-5 rounded-xl border border-teal/30 bg-teal/10 p-3.5 text-sm"><b className="font-medium text-[#0e9f7a]">In practice:</b> Keep the documentary requirements clear enough that a colleague can review the presentation consistently.</div></div><button className="btn-primary mt-8"><Icon name="check" /> Mark as read</button></article></div>;
}

